# Code owners for Blockcast/paperclip.
#
# GitHub silently ignores any entry naming an account without write access to
# this repository. Such a rule parses fine but enforces nothing — no auto-requested
# review, no code-owner gate. Verify after editing:
#
#   gh api "repos/Blockcast/paperclip/codeowners/errors?ref=master"   # expect {"errors":[]}
#
# Replace @kkroo if a different maintainer or team should own a surface. Prefer an
# org team over a single human once one exists.

.github/** @kkroo
scripts/release*.sh @kkroo
scripts/release-*.mjs @kkroo
scripts/create-github-release.sh @kkroo
scripts/rollback-latest.sh @kkroo
doc/RELEASING.md @kkroo
doc/PUBLISHING.md @kkroo
doc/RELEASE-AUTOMATION-SETUP.md @kkroo
skills/** @kkroo

# Package files — dependency changes require review
# package.json matches recursively at all depths (covers root + all workspaces)
package.json @kkroo
pnpm-lock.yaml @kkroo
pnpm-workspace.yaml @kkroo
.npmrc @kkroo

# Secret-scan configuration — the files that can SILENCE the scanner (PEN-6393).
#
# `.github/**` above owns the `secret_scan` lane that RUNS gitleaks. These entries
# own the files that can switch it off, which that rule does not reach: gitleaks
# resolves its config from the working tree, so an allowlist entry added by a PR
# takes effect on that same PR, and the lane's own failure comment tells the
# author to add one. Review of that diff is the only thing enforcing the policy
# `.gitleaks.toml`'s header states.
#
# `.gitleaksignore` is a second silencing channel that needs no config edit:
# gitleaks v8.28.0 — the version the lane pins — auto-loads it from `--source`
# and drops findings by fingerprint. No such file exists yet; the rule is here so
# the PR that first adds one draws a reviewer. `.gitleaks-staging.toml` is the
# warn-only staging config (PEN-6280) and is likewise not on master yet.
#
# Listed by exact name rather than a glob: a config under a NEW name is not
# covered — add a line here when you add one.
#
# What this buys, and what it does not: GitHub does not enforce CODEOWNERS on
# this repository (no `require_code_owner_review`), so these entries are not a
# merge gate. They auto-request @kkroo and the landing routine then holds the PR
# on that outstanding request rather than enqueuing it. That is a hold plus a
# named reviewer — not guaranteed scrutiny, and not a blocking check.
.gitleaks.toml @kkroo
.gitleaks-staging.toml @kkroo
.gitleaksignore @kkroo
